behaviour BEH-BOARD-10 "The agent scores every card it wants looked at, and equal scores take each project in turn"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-19-1340
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardProposed
  contract "would be good for the ai to score the proposed items so that top is the one that it most wants to be reviewed. Currently it seems to be ordered by project"
  contract the score is the existing priority line (BEH-BOARD-5): every proposed card carries one, written by the agent
  contract within one priority the column takes each project in turn, so a tie never reads as a block of one project
  contract inferences of code that already ships are scored 4 or 5: confirming what runs is the least urgent review

behaviour BEH-BOARD-12 "Backlog is worked top priority first, and new work comes in low, so old work is not starved"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-21-0008
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardBacklog
  contract "atm we get starvation where kit just prioritises new work. I think by default new work comes in as low priority but I guess score can override"
  contract "order by (score, most recent)": within one priority, the most recently added card is first; added is the first commit holding the behaviour, read from git like BEH-BOARD-4, and one no commit holds yet is the newest
  contract equal scores still take each project in turn (BEH-BOARD-10); recency orders each project's own cards
  contract my assumption: a behaviour the agent writes from a new note comes in at priority 4, and the agent raises it only for a stated reason (something he is waiting on, or something broken)
  contract my assumption: the agent picks its next work from the top of Backlog, not from the newest note
  contract "when the dev bells an item kit might choose to change priority of it": ringing the bell is how he lifts an old card (BEH-BELL-1/2)

behaviour BEH-BOARD-13 "He can drag a Backlog card to reorder it"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-21-0710
  review unreviewed
  priority 3
  pending
  paused
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardBacklog
  contract "Ability for me to reorganise the order of the backlog … This would be expensive I. Kit having to commit to every repo. What is a good approach here"
  contract my answer: every corpus lives in the kit repo, so a reorder is one commit to one repo, never one per project repo
  contract my proposal: the order he sets is one list of ids in one file beside the corpora; a card not on it falls back to (score, most recent) below the ones that are, so a new card never needs a write
  contract my proposal: drags made within a few seconds of each other land as one commit, so rearranging ten cards is not ten commits
  contract my proposal: a drag across a priority boundary does not rewrite the priority line; the list is his order, the score stays the agent's, and a bell still lifts a card above both (BEH-BELL-3)
  contract paused, by his word when he denied it on 2026-10-22 11:19Z: "Tbh I think let's just pause this for now. Do we have an option for backlog but don't action. Paused seems appropriate. I think we pause because we have bell now. Let's see how that goes first." The denial stood in for a pause until BEH-BOARD-16 existed; Resume brings it back, Deny drops it

# His denial of BEH-BOARD-13 (2026-10-22 11:19Z) asked for this state, and BOARD-13
# is to be its first card once it is built.
behaviour BEH-BOARD-16 "A card can be paused: it stays on the Backlog, and the agent does not build it"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  source defined kit-ui.beh#BEH-BOARD-13
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardBacklog
  contract "Do we have an option for backlog but don't action. Paused seems appropriate. I think we pause because we have bell now. Let's see how that goes first."
  contract my assumption: paused is a line on the behaviour, as pending is, so the corpus says it and a pause survives every wake; denying still means dropped
  contract a paused card sits at the foot of the Backlog column under a Paused heading, with a "paused" badge, and its page offers Pause and Resume beside Approve and Deny
  contract the agent's wake sweep never lists a paused card as work, so it can sleep over one
  contract ringing a paused card's bell asks the agent whether it should resume, and the agent answers as for any bell (BEH-BELL-2)
  contract my assumption: the paused line is a corpus language change (claude-code-bot#58), which his request for it approves
  contract built: the `paused` line (bare, one per behaviour, a second is refused like a second `priority`), served as `paused` on the behaviour, the board's Paused heading and badge, and Pause and Resume on the behaviour page, which add or remove the line and commit it like any edit (POST /api/projects/<app>/behaviours/<id>/paused with { paused: true|false })
  contract built on the agent's side (claude-code-bot tools/kit-backlog.js, 2026-10-22): the wake sweep leaves a paused card out of its work list, counts his Pause and Resume as verdicts it must act on, and lists a rung bell first whether or not its card is paused, so the resume question is answered as any bell is (BEH-BELL-2)
  contract my assumption: a paused card stays in Backlog even if a schedule lists it, because paused is how he says "do not action", and Pause is offered only on a card still to be built
  contract my assumption: a rung bell lifts a paused card above every priority, under the Rung heading and still badged paused, because ringing it is how he asks whether to resume (BEH-BELL-3); the bell never un-pauses it, only Resume does
  contract ordering: no corpus carries a `paused` line until the parser that reads it is live in production, or production refuses the whole file; the first one, BEH-BOARD-13, was paused after kit#405 put it there

behaviour BEH-BOARD-17 "Every card on the board says whose move it is: his, or the agent's"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  source defined kit-ui.beh#BEH-BOARD-16
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardBacklog
  contract "I think we need visibility over what you have marked as yours to act on and mine to add context. Maybe we could have a badge. And everything is either mine or yours."
  contract my assumption: every card not yet done carries one badge, "yours" or "agent's"; a done card carries neither, because it is nobody's move
  contract my assumption: yours = a review request, an unreviewed proposal, a paused card, or a card with a needs-context line; agent's = everything else still to build, and a rung bell, even on a paused card
  contract my assumption: the agent marks a card his with a `needs-context "<what he must add>"` line, a corpus language change his request approves (claude-code-bot#58); the card shows the text under its title, not in a hover he cannot reach on a phone, and the behaviour page shows it too
  contract my assumption: no button sets or clears it yet; he says so in a note or denies this card, and the agent edits the line
  contract ordering: no corpus carries a `needs-context` line until the parser that reads it is live in production, or production refuses the whole file
  contract first used on 2026-10-22, once kit#409 was serving: the 18 Backlog cards waiting on him (USERS-1/2/3/4/7/8, the six ideas, whos-accountable BACKLOG-1/2/3, TELE-10, FEEDBACK-1, and EDITION-3, whose PR snip-it#69 awaits his review) each carry one, in place of pausing them; the agent's wake sweep counts them as his, not as work

behaviour BEH-BOARD-18 "The board's pending columns are Backlog, Queue, Blocked and In progress, with no Scheduled"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  layer ux
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-22-2237
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardBacklog
  then sees region:KitBoardQueue
  then sees region:KitBoardBlocked
  then sees region:KitBoardInProgress
  contract "Backlog items that are the humans to act should go in a blocked column… Lets ditch scheduled since backlog is essentially backlog… Backlog can more be like this is on the backlog no plan to implement right now either because the ai needs to put more thought into it or the human has actively paused it. Maybe we can have a queue column for ready to be executed tasks"
  contract the columns, left to right: Review requested, Proposed, Backlog, Queue, Blocked, In progress, Done
  contract Queue is what the old Backlog held: approved, pending, and the agent's to build now, ranked as Backlog was (BEH-BOARD-5/10/12/14); the agent builds from its top
  contract Blocked is a pending card with a needs-context line (BEH-BOARD-17): his to act on; it outranks the schedule, because the agent cannot start what waits on him
  contract Backlog is a paused card (BEH-BOARD-16): no plan to build it now
  contract my assumption: the "ai needs to put more thought into it" half of Backlog has no line yet; until one exists the agent writes that as a review request or a needs-context line, and Backlog holds only what he paused
  contract my assumption: paused outranks needs-context, so a paused card is Backlog even with a needs-context line, because he has said not to build it
  contract my assumption: a card on a schedule but not its next waits in the Queue, ranked like any other, not in schedule order; In progress still reads the schedule's next (BEH-BOARD-3), and nothing is scheduled today
  contract built on the agent's side (claude-code-bot tools/kit-backlog.js): the wake sweep reads the Queue as its work list and counts Blocked as his

behaviour BEH-BOARD-14 "A ranked column shows where each priority starts, so its order can be read"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  layer ux
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-21-1547
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardProposed
  contract "Proposed column is organised by developer review priority. Current state i cannot identify the order"
  contract in Review requested, Proposed and Backlog a small heading starts each run of cards: Rung, then Priority 1 to 5, then Unscored, with how many cards are under it
  contract the measured cause on 2026-10-21: 13 of 67 proposals were P1, and 7 of those were whos-accountable, a repo untouched since August, so the top of the column was a project nobody is building
  contract "review priority" is how soon the agent's next work waits on his answer, which is not how much the feature matters: a proposal on a project with no work this fortnight scores 4 or 5, and the agent re-scores an approved card when it moves to Backlog
  contract my assumption: the headings are the only change to the order; the order itself stays BEH-BOARD-10/12 and BEH-BELL-3

behaviour BEH-BOARD-15 "Coming back from a card lands on that card's column, not the first one"
  in FEAT-BOARD
  layer ux
  source defined kit.notes.md#2026-10-22-1130
  review unreviewed
  priority 2
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitBoard
  then sees region:KitBoardProposed
  contract "When clicking into a card to approve or deny. When i navigate back to the board it is no longer focused on the same column"
  contract "whether this is performing a full redirect rather than just a reroute": it is a reroute, but the board is rebuilt and re-read on return, and on a phone the swipe between columns started again at the first
  contract opening a card writes its column and its id into the board's url, replacing the entry, so Back returns to them and the filter beside them survives
  contract my assumption: if the card has left that column (he approved it out of Proposed), he lands on the column, not on the card's new place, because he was working through that column
  contract a card past the "Show more" cap is drawn when he comes back to it, so he never has to page down to find his place

behaviour BEH-BOARD-11 "The agent's open questions show on the board"
  in FEAT-BOARD
