behaviour BEH-USERS-1 "An early-access user signs in with a username and password James made, then connects their GitHub"
  in FEAT-USER-SIGNUP
  source inferred kit.notes.md#2026-10-19-1721
  review approved
  actor engineer
  when opens page:KitProjects
  then sees region:KitMain
  contract his list: "works with the user's own GitHub (OAuth and a GitHub App, with a smooth sign-up)"
  contract his answer (kit.notes.md#2026-10-23-0638): "let's have a username and password sign in for early access. And then you integrate your GitHub. Then maybe later for general release it's just a GitHub sign in"
  contract early access is invite-only because James creates each account himself; there is no open sign-up, no GitHub allowlist and no code word (he weighed both in kit.notes.md#2026-10-23-0635 and dropped them)
  contract a user can change the password James gave them
  contract once signed in, the user connects their GitHub by installing Kit's GitHub App, which is what reads and writes their repos
  contract later, for general release, signing in with GitHub replaces the username and password; that is a separate behaviour, not this one
  contract my assumption: he creates each account inside Kit, signed in as himself, rather than with a command run in the cluster
  contract my assumption: he signs in as before, with KIT_PASSWORD and no name or the name james; his password is changed where Kit is deployed, not in Kit
  contract my assumption: a user name is 2 to 32 lower-case letters, digits, '-' or '_'; a password has at least 12 characters and is stored only as a PBKDF2-SHA256 hash
  contract my assumption: accounts live in the database Kit runs (the one BEH-USERS-4 names); until that database exists, Kit keeps its single password and no account can be made
  contract his answer (kit.notes.md#2026-10-23-0639, and kit#423 at 08:50Z): Kit may use a database; he approved it on BEH-USERS-4, so accounts need no separate approval
  contract my assumption: connecting is two steps on GitHub, installing Kit's App and then authorising it; Kit records only the installation on the personal account of the user who authorised, never the id on the install redirect (GitHub warns it can be forged) and never an organisation's (any member can reach one); the user's own token is used for that and revoked, never kept
  contract my assumption: one installation belongs to one Kit user, and the installation Kit itself runs on is never a user's
  contract my assumption: it switches on when Kit's App has an OAuth client (KIT_GITHUB_APP_SLUG, KIT_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID, KIT_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET) and Kit's page as its setup and callback URL; until James sets those, no user is offered it
  contract his answer (kit#431, 2026-10-23 22:03Z): "I think we make a new public GitHub app"; so users install a new public App named Kit, and the bot's own App stays private and keeps running Kit
  contract built: kit#431 (merged 2026-10-23); kit#433 has the chart read the App's slug, client id and secret from a hand-made kit-github-connect secret, and connecting stays off until James creates that App and secret

